{"detail": {"id": "hoids-hop8-3", "book": "HoIDS", "chapter": 8, "exercise_number": "Hands-On Problem 8.3", "title": "Gradient Descent \u2014 petroleum rock samples", "category": "Gradient descent", "source_page": "p. 232", "kind": "computational", "required_dataset": "rock", "origin": "textbook", "description": "Using 48 rock samples (area, peri, shape, perm): first create a linear model and check whether perm has a linear relationship with the other three attributes; next use gradient descent to find the optimal intercept and gradient.", "concepts": ["multiple linear regression", "gradient descent (multivariate)", "feature scaling", "R\u00b2"], "understand": "With three predictors, \u03b8 has four values. Gradient descent updates all of them simultaneously using eq. 8.7.", "questions": [{"id": "1", "prompt": "Fit a linear model perm ~ area + peri + shape and judge linearity.", "answer": null, "explanation": ""}, {"id": "2", "prompt": "Use gradient descent to find the optimal intercept and coefficients.", "answer": null, "explanation": ""}], "interpretation": "Both methods give the same coefficients; the moderate R\u00b2 and skewed perm suggest a transformed model (e.g. log(perm)) could fit better.", "common_mistakes": ["Running gradient descent on unscaled features (area \u2248 10\u2074, shape \u2248 0.1) \u2014 one \u03b1 cannot suit both.", "Reading coefficient size as importance when features are on different scales."], "python_code": "import numpy as np, pandas as pd\nrock = pd.read_csv(\"rock.csv\")                       # R datasets::rock\nX, y = rock[[\"area\", \"peri\", \"shape\"]].to_numpy(float), rock[\"perm\"].to_numpy(float)\nmu, sd = X.mean(0), X.std(0)\nZ = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(y)), (X - mu) / sd]) # x0 = 1, scaled features\ntheta, alpha = np.zeros(4), 0.1\nfor i in range(5000):\n    theta -= alpha * Z.T @ (Z @ theta - y) / len(y)   # eq. 8.7 for all j at once\ncoef = theta[1:] / sd; intercept = theta[0] - np.sum(theta[1:] * mu / sd)\nprint(intercept, coef)\n", "parameters": {"learning_rate": 0.1, "iterations": 5000}, "notes": ["This is an end-of-chapter Hands-On Problem, NOT a Try It Yourself (Chapter 8 contains only TiY 8.1).", "The rock data is R's built-in `datasets::rock`, which matches the description (48 samples, BP Research, Ronit Katz)."]}, "solve": {"answers": [{"id": "1", "answer": {"intercept": 485.61797447128043, "coefficients": {"area": 0.09133378912956669, "peri": -0.3440246028712843, "shape": 899.0692598434199}, "r2": 0.7044102825997469, "correlations_with_perm": {"area": -0.39663702178399685, "peri": -0.7387157730582904, "shape": 0.5567208329520545}}, "explanation": "OLS: perm = 485.62 + 0.0913\u00b7area + -0.3440\u00b7peri + 899.0693\u00b7shape. R\u00b2 = 0.704: a linear model explains about 70% of the variation in permeability, so the relationship is only partly linear (perm is very skewed: values from 6.3 to 1300)."}, {"id": "2", "answer": {"intercept": 485.61791969746804, "coefficients": {"area": 0.09133373339705966, "peri": -0.34402448485394976, "shape": 899.0698962938425}, "iterations_run": 1068, "stop_reason": "converged"}, "explanation": "Gradient descent on standardized features (Shah eq. 8.7) converges to the same coefficients as OLS once the cost stops changing."}], "ols_metrics": {"n": 48, "mae": 158.89699256433227, "mse": 55479.6377851425, "rmse": 235.54115942896794, "r2": 0.7044102825997469, "ss_res": 2663022.61368684, "ss_tot": 9009185.559999999}, "gradient_descent": {"theta_standardized": [415.4499999999997, 242.55910194518754, -487.3690069150805, 74.28305685303584], "intercept": 485.61791969746804, "coefficients": [0.09133373339705966, -0.34402448485394976, 899.0698962938425], "final_cost": 27739.81889257481, "iterations_run": 1068, "stop_reason": "converged", "history": [{"iteration": 1, "theta": [0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0], "cost": 180145.03416666668, "gradient": [-415.45, 171.8364910958101, 320.0365053885319, -241.1901794344238]}, {"iteration": 10, "theta": [254.49615784495